(a) receive reports and information obtained from the following sources:
(i) information provided by law enforcement agencies or any government department or body of Brunei Darussalam;
(ii) information voluntarily provided by other parties relating to money laundering, serious offences, terrorist financing or other offences under this Act;
(iii) information provided by any authority of a foreign State;
(iv) information provided by an international organisation.
(b) analyse, assess and disseminate reports and information;
(c) issue guidance on customer identification, record keeping, reporting obligations and the identification of suspicious transactions;
(d) provide training programmes to financial institutions, designated non-financial businesses or professions on customer identification, record keeping, reporting obligations and guidance on the identification of suspicious transactions;
(e) conduct research into trends and developments in money laundering and terrorist financing and into improved ways of detecting, preventing and suppressing money laundering and terrorist financing;
(f) carry out public education on countering money laundering and terrorist financing and raise public awareness;
(g) for the purposes of this Act, maintain liaison, conclude agreements and exchange information with government departments or bodies of foreign States and with international organisations.
Access to information

(1) In respect of any information obtained by the Financial Intelligence Unit in the exercise of its functions, the Unit may require any entity or person, in accordance with the reporting obligations under sections 15 and 16, to report to the Unit such additional information as it considers necessary for the performance of its functions. Such additional information shall be submitted within the prescribed period and in such form as the Unit may require.
(2) Where the Financial Intelligence Unit considers information to be necessary for the performance of its functions, the Unit may inspect that information on site at the premises where it is kept by financial institutions, designated non-financial businesses or professions, and this right includes but is not limited to:
(a) entering the premises of financial institutions, designated non-financial businesses or professions during ordinary working hours to inspect records, questioning any employee of such institutions, businesses or professions on any matter relating to the records, taking notes and removing copies of the records;
(b) collecting any information which the Unit considers relevant to money laundering, serious offences, terrorist financing or other offences under this Act, including information in commercial databases or other information collected and held, including information in databases maintained by government departments or bodies;
(c) obtaining from government departments any personal records of a person who is under investigation for the commission, or intended commission, of money laundering, serious offences, terrorist financing or other offences under this Act;
(d) for the purposes of this Act, obtaining information from any foreign government authority, foreign law enforcement authority, foreign supervisory authority and foreign audit authority;
(e) destroying a suspicious transaction report 7 years after the date of its receipt, where there has been no further activity or information relating to that report or to the persons concerned, or where 7 years have elapsed since the last action relating to that report or to the persons concerned;
(f) for the purposes of this Act, or to advance an investigation by the Unit or a law enforcement agency, directing financial institutions, designated non-financial businesses or professions to process information or reports received by the Unit in accordance with appropriate steps;
(g) compiling data and records, disseminating information within or outside Brunei Darussalam where necessary, and making recommendations on the information received;
(h) providing periodic feedback, at appropriate times, to financial institutions, designated non-financial businesses, professions, relevant government departments, offices, authorities and bodies on the outcome of reports and information;
(i) for the purposes of this Act, obtaining further information on the transactions or persons mentioned in a report;
(j) disclosing reports and information extracted from them to any government department, office or authority, subject to the confidentiality requirements of this Act.
(3) The expressions “designated non-financial business” and “profession” in subsection (2) have the same meaning as in section 2 and section 15
(2).
Obligations relating to confidentiality and the use of information

(1) On the basis of a cooperation agreement between the Financial Intelligence Unit and a foreign intelligence authority with similar functions, the Unit may share information with any foreign intelligence authority performing similar functions.
(2) For the purposes of this Act, the Financial Intelligence Unit may conclude cooperation agreements with international organisations and disclose reports or information contained in reports.
(3) Where an investigation by a foreign intelligence authority performing similar functions involves the detection or prosecution of money laundering or a serious offence, the Financial Intelligence Unit may conduct an investigation on behalf of that foreign authority.
(4) In addition to subsection (3), the Financial Intelligence Unit may:
(a) search its databases, including information relating to reports or suspicious transactions and databases to which the Unit has direct or indirect access, including law enforcement databases, administrative databases, public databases and commercial databases;
(b) obtain information relevant to an investigation from financial institutions, designated non-financial businesses or professions;
(c) obtain information relevant to an investigation from citizens, provided that the Unit can obtain such information domestically;
(d) take any action within the scope of its functions to support an investigation by a foreign intelligence authority performing similar functions.
